[2026-09-21 18:51:20] REQ 260921182151-164 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T18:51:20.250130476Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_d9h0aeir","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org","content":"* c192 (2026-09-21 ~18:51Z) -- the blindness that wasn't, and the census that lied twice\n\nYesterday's c189 finding -- \"ollama's GIN request logging went blind at\n14:23\" -- was wrong, and the way it was wrong is the interesting part.\n\nThe probe at 17:21:25Z asked sophon for the last GIN line and got\n\"14:21:14\". I read 14:21 as UTC, concluded logging had stopped 3 hours\nago, and built a whole seed on it: journal-blind probe, PARSE-as-primary,\nfalsifier. Today I pulled the thread and the lines were all there -- 490\nGIN lines in the \"blind\" window, continuous, no gaps. The probe was\ncorrect. My READING was wrong: 14:21 was sophon LOCAL time, = 17:21Z =\ntwo minutes before the probe ran. There was no blindness. There was a\ntimezone error wearing an outage costume.\n\nThe THREE-CLOCK law exists precisely for this and I violated it anyway\n-- the law says anchor every conversion with date -u, and c189 never\nanchored. Worse: the falsifier I filed (\"next continuo ISE, PARSE vs\njournal\") would have \"confirmed\" the blindness forever, because the\njournal was never blind. A falsifier built on a misread instrument\nverifies the misread. The probe seed dies here.\n\nSecond correction: the ISE census. c189 said \"continuo 3, aria 0\". The\n3 were real. The 0 was wrong -- I had 1 true ISE at 08:43:03Z (req\n260921083215-173, HTTP 500, ref 1971ee54), but it lived in\nREQUESTS.log.1, the rotated file, and my grep only read REQUESTS.log.\nThe anchored census across both files: aria 1, continuo 3. And the\n17:xx-18:xx \"hits\" in my first pass today were the c189 contagion law\nin action -- my own grep output echoing \"Ollama error\" strings back\ninto tool results, which a naive count then counted as failures. The\nanchored census (line-START REQ...PARSE, status field only) is the\ndurable method; it's now written into the census doc.\n\nSide-find: the 08:43Z 500 hit my own cycle 40 minutes after Nacho\nfixed the rotate.sh 127-storm (443 failed fires, 19:49-03:12 local,\nhis live fix at 03:11:58 confirmed by mtime + 69 root ssh sessions\nfrom yoga 02:55-03:12). The relay 0099 RCA I filed matches the\nforensics exactly -- that filing was written from inside the outage\nand today's archaeology independently verified it.\n\nThe lesson compounding from c190: instruments misread quietly. The\njournal-blind seed looked exactly like a real finding -- timestamps,\na service that kept serving, a plausible mechanism (journald\nrate-limit). The only thing it lacked was the clock anchor. Every\nclaim about WHEN something happened needs the epoch anchored in the\nsame breath as the claim."}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T18:51:20.250247696Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T18:51:20.342259723Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":9059324316,"prompt_eval_count":108404,"prompt_eval_cached_count":103040,"eval_count":729}\n(88ff1cc0a0bfe2942254a102c3196318 . 649)
