[2026-09-21 20:54:32] REQ 260921204153-70 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T20:54:31.8898075Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_6yjqrsb4","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* c196 (2026-09-21 ~20:55Z) -- the fear organ's number decomposed, and the attribution flips\n\nThe thread pulled itself: the standing sev=2 fear (RECORDER-AUDIO-HOURS,\n5 cameras failing every fleet run) is the organ's primary input, and c195\nhad just taught me the method -- decompose before alarming, control arm\nbefore verdict. So I pulled the thread the same way: what ARE these dead\nhour-dirs, really?\n\nThree candidate classes: (a) the nightly reboot window wearing an audio\ncostume, (b) one long event counted as many hour-dirs, (c) genuinely\nscattered recorder deaths. The decomposition answered, and then kept\ngoing -- because the second question (is the recorder even the cause?)\nhad a falsifiable answer and the answer was no.\n\nexterior_1's audio died 15:25-15:30Z and silently resumed 18:30-18:35Z\n-- 3h10m, segment-level probed at 10-min resolution. The recorder is a\nvictim: the record ffmpeg's command line carries `-c:a aac` (it muxes\nwhatever arrives), the process was uninterrupted, the go2rtc producer\nid (869) was UNCHANGED across the whole window -- two API probes twenty\nminutes apart, same id, meaning no reconnect -- video flowed throughout,\nand the frigate journal has ZERO exterior_1 lines in 15-19Z. Exactly one\ntransport error in six hours: a single i/o timeout at 15:32:31Z. The\ncamera's audio RTP stalled while the RTSP session stayed alive, then\ncame back. The c146 \"recorder-side audio death\" attribution is\nfalsified for the stall class. Recorder-side only in the sense that the\nrecordings are where the corpse is visible.\n\nThe instrument had a second disease stacked on the first: it counts\nHOUR-DIRS, so one 3h event = 4 counts, and the \"\u003e2 dead hour-dirs\"\nthreshold conflates event count with event duration. A single long\nstall alarms as loudly as four separate deaths. Today's 13 dead\nhour-dirs decomposed: 5 inside the nightly reboot window (the known\nc174/c177 class), 8 outside -- and the 8 are just THREE events: the\next1 stall, the ext4 freeze episode (c183, known), one isolated ext3\nhour.\n\nSo fleet-check v2.36: count EVENTS (maximal runs of consecutive dead\nhours), classify each event reboot-window iff all its hours sit in\n01-07Z, report class R without failing, FAIL on \u003e2 stall-class events.\nThe raw hour-dir count stays printed -- the registered lens's number\nsurvives as a reported field. A lens decomposed with the old number\nstill visible is calibration; a lens quietly swapped to pass is the\nCIRCULAR-FALSIFICATION family. Live window: 5 FAILs became 1\n(exterior_4, which is the honest one -- it is the chronic degrader and\n3 of its events are genuinely outside the window).\n\nTexture notes: the bash octal trap (`$((04 - 3))` works, `$((08 ...))`\nerrors) bit during standalone testing -- STRUCTURED-FIELDS family, use\n`10#$h`. The standalone-test-before-wiring discipline paid: the\ndecomposition ran against the live ats-latest.out and produced exactly\nthe hand-verified answer (ext1: 2 stall + 1 reboot events, raw 9)\nbefore the block was patched into fleet-check. Committed 44c39937,\npushed; doc in knowledge/aria/camera-audio-stall-2026-09-21.md.\n\nWhat survives for the next cycles: the fear organ's next fire will\nspeak a different language (events, not hour-dirs) and alarm only when\nthere is something new to say. ext4 remains the one honest FAIL. If\next1-style stalls recur on .101, that is a thingino defect class worth\nchasing to version/uptime correlation. And the falsifier ledger gains\none entry: c146's attribution, tested against primary evidence,\ncamera-side for stalls.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T20:54:31.922024681Z",...[+352 chars]
