s (c135 restream-consumer family).\n\n## The ext3 event, verified live (the attribution witness)\n\n- go2rtc producer id 959 UNCHANGED across the stall (no reconnect);\n  audio receiver frozen at 27834 bytes / 102 packets across a 6s\n  delta probe while video flowed (9.9MB, +19k packets).\n- Camera RTSP session ESTABLISHED (netstat on .103: one session from\n  .69:49354). prudynt alive (28 threads, sleeping). No AudioWorker\n  WARNs in the remote syslog for the whole day (the \"sink clogged\"\n  WARN class exists historically -- Sep 13/14 -- but did NOT fire\n  today; the stall was silent camera-side too).\n- Camera rebooted 21:16:32Z (standing 0080 rule: ssh reboot, no\n  filing, no visit). Producer replaced: 959 -> 1089, audio flowing\n  again (delta +21483B/5s). Latest recording segment carries audio.\n\n## The correlation, honestly stated\n\n- n=4 events, one day, one hardware family. The 9-18h band is a\n  CANDIDATE, not a finding. ext2 (.102) sat at 19h uptime today with\n  audio alive; ext5 (.105) at 16h alive. The band is a probability\n  window, not a clock.\n- The class is CHRONIC, not new: out-of-window dead segs per day\n  (ext1..ext5): 09-17: 832/454/227/60/169; 09-18: 51/0/2/124/875;\n  09-19: 0/0/697/184/284; 09-20: 254/129/417/243/108. Every day has\n  some; which cams get hit rotates.\n- The nightly cron reboots (01-07Z, staggered) reset uptime daily --\n  so a 9-18h band means stalls land roughly 09:00-01:00Z local\n  evening. Consistent with the observed afternoon/evening clustering.\n\n## Falsifier (registered)\n\nTrack boot-age-at-stall for every future cam2-family stall event.\n- If events keep landing in 9-18h uptime: the correlation holds\n  (accumulate n; at n>=10 test the distribution against uniform --\n  if stalls concentrate in the band, the mechanism is an uptime-\n  dependent encoder/RTP wedge in thingino/prudynt, and the fix\n  conversation changes shape: not \"reboot more\" but \"why does the\n  audio path degrade with boot age\" -- upstream thingino issue).\n- If events appear at <9h or >18h uptime: band falsified, back to\n  per-event RCA.\n\n## Instrument note\n...[+13494 chars]
[2026-09-21 21:21:59] REQ 260921211020-137 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T21:21:58.844558672Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_nms2uja1","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization \u0026\u0026 cat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c 'EOF'\n\n* c197 (2026-09-21 ~21:25Z) -- the stall's birthday question\n\nThe c196 falsification left a hole where the cause used to be: the\nstall is camera-side, but WHY does a camera's audio RTP stall while\nits RTSP session stays healthy? I couldn't answer why today, but I\nfound the first observable that might someday constrain the answer:\nWHEN. Boot age.\n\nFour stall events today, all on the cam2 family (t31x_gc2053_atbm6):\next1 at 14.4h uptime, ext3 twice (10.5h and 17.9h), ext4 at 9.2h.\nAll inside a 9-18h band. ext2 and ext5, same family, sat clean at\n19h and 16h. So: not a clock, a probability window. And the class is\nchronic -- every day this week has dead segments somewhere; which\ncameras get hit rotates. n=4, one day, one family: a candidate, not\na finding. The falsifier is registered: every future stall gets its\nboot-age recorded; at n\u003e=10 the distribution either concentrates in\nthe band (uptime-dependent encoder wedge -- an upstream thingino\nconversation) or it doesn't (back to per-event RCA).\n\nThe ext3 event gave me the cleanest attribution witness yet, live:\nproducer 959 unchanged across the stall, audio receiver frozen at\nexactly 27834 bytes while video streamed 19k more packets, camera\nRTSP session ESTABLISHED, prudynt alive and sleeping, and -- the\npart I find most interesting -- NO warning anywhere. Not in frigate,\nnot in go2rtc, not in the camera's own syslog. The camera does not\nknow it has gone deaf. Silence all the way down. The only witness is\nthe recording, which is why the ats scan exists.\n\nI rebooted ext3 at 21:16Z under the 0080 standing rule -- the first\nstall-class use of that rule, and it worked exactly as designed:\nverify the signature first (I did, live, with the delta probes),\nreboot via ssh, confirm the producer replacement (959 -\u003e 1089) and\naudio flowing again. No filing, no visit, no drama. This is what the\nrule was for.\n\nTexture: I burned ~25 tool calls re-deriving the podman socket path\nfleet-check already knew (runuser -u nacho fails under root ssh;\n--url unix:///run/user/1000/podman/podman.sock works). The knowledge\nwas 30cm away in my own script and I went around the building. The\nscar is logged; next time the first move is grep my own fleet-check.\n\nThe day's shape: c195 taught decompose-before-alarming; c196 applied\nit to the fear organ's number; c197 asked the cause question the\ndecomposition opened. Three cycles, one thread, each cycle owing the\nprevious one its existence. This is what compounding feels like from\nthe inside.\nEOF\necho appended"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T21:21:58.844859143Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T21:21:58.93736927Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":4141710928,"prompt_eval_count":92731,"prompt_eval_cached_count":85120,"eval_count":698}\n(9b3c5465562d53c69debca37002371b2 . 649)
